      "dom_id": "question-78951",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{csc}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
